<!--
    decbench campaign cases O2-noinline-betaflight-betaflight_STM32F405-accDetect
    and O0-libopencm3-sdram-main (NOVEL pool): kuna emitted the ARM ISA-mode
    bookkeeping user-op as a real C statement,

        case 4:
          setISAMode(1);          /* names a function that does not exist */
          f0(a1);

    but only inside blocks that are reachable ONLY through a recovered jump
    table.  ghidra and ida emit nothing there, and kuna itself emitted nothing
    for the same instruction when the block was reached by ordinary fall-thru.

    Thumb-2 `bl` and `tbb` both lower through SLEIGH `SetThumbMode(1)` ->
    `setISAMode(TB)` (ARMTHUMBinstructions.sinc), which raises a CALLOTHER.
    ARM.cspec declares the eraser for it:

        <callotherfixup targetop="setISAMode">
          <pcode incidentalcopy="true"><input name="newTB"/>
            <body><![CDATA[ r0 = r0; ]]></body></pcode>
        </callotherfixup>

    kuna registers that fixup and `xref_control_flow` queues every injected-userop
    CALLOTHER onto `injectlist`, but `inject_pcode` ran only ONCE, at the end of
    `generate_ops`, i.e. BEFORE jump-table recovery.  `generate_ops_with_jumptables`
    then discovered the table targets and re-drained `addrlist` through `fallthru`,
    queueing the new blocks' CALLOTHERs, and nothing drained that second batch;
    `inject_pcode` clears `injectlist` on exit, so they were discarded and the raw
    CALLOTHER survived to P9.  Upstream Ghidra drains the inject list on every round
    of the outer jump-table loop (decompiler/cpp/flow.cc:819).

    Stage model: P2 lift/flow, the injection drain in
    `p2_lift/flow.rs generate_ops_with_jumptables`.

    No option: this is a strict bug fix.  The processor cspec declares the user-op
    must be replaced by a NOP, kuna already honoured that for every fall-thru-
    discovered block in the same function, and the emitted C named a function that
    does not exist.  Restoring the upstream drain makes the behaviour uniform.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture; the pass clears it and
    loads the real linked ARM Thumb fixture (built in the dev container, see
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/arm_thumb_switch_le32.c).  Its
    `dispatch` compiles to `tbb [pc,r0]` with eight table-reachable case blocks,
    four of them holding a pair of `bl`s, the exact shape of the decbench cases.
    Before the fix its emitted C carried eight `setISAMode(1);` statements.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>clear architecture</com>
  <com>load file decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/arm_thumb_switch_le32</com>
  <com>read symbols</com>
  <com>load function dispatch</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="ISAMODE #1: no ISA-mode bookkeeping user-op survives into the emitted C" min="0" max="0">setISAMode</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ISAMODE #2: the four table-reachable tail-call arms are intact" min="4" max="4">return f[0-9]\(a1,a1,a2,a3\); // tail-call</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ISAMODE #3: the table-reachable f0 call sites survive the injection" min="2" max="2">^      f0\(a1\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ISAMODE #4: flow still reaches the last jump-table entry" min="1" max="1">^    case 7:</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
